body:
> 1. What's the use case for this? You claim you need to book-keep top layer items, I'm curious as to why, like what's the concrete thing you're doing here?

I was solving an issue for a client recently (large German newspaper, my scenario involves the main landing page and a gazillion of subdomains, and an army of third-party contributions that need to play nicely together in the page): the cookie consent and advertising consent modal dialog must be displayed to the user top-most (z-layering-wise). Now on some subdomain pages the third-party content opened another modal dialog as part of its own legitimate uses-case. Which would end up ontop of the cookie dialog. The order was indeterministic because of requests that ran prior to opening the dialogs; and I didn't want to introduce coupling between the subdomain feature and my cookie dialog.
So I solved it by having a watcher while my cookie dialog is open that would detect any modal dialog opening (I listened for the `focus` event on the root); and bringing my dialog to the front by closing and opening it again. Client happy, feature shipped.
Now I'm aware that my solution while solving the immediate case doesn't solve the problem generally: still some third-party script could open a popover, which would still end up displayed ontop of my cookie dialog. It would be inert but visible. And any other top-layer content (that is not a modal dialog) would create the same problem.
So I currently try to find a general solution by developing (in my spare time, open source) a general top-layer observer; that would allow myself and other developers to get notified whenever the top-layer content changes, allow to get the top-layer stacking order, and to have some kind of information of what nature the top layer items are (modal dialog, popover, select's picker dropdown, ...)

> 2. How do you handle other ambiguities like click events, focus events, input events, change events and so on? e.g. a change event can come from radio controls or inputs or textareas or select, and each one needs to do a different thing.

I shamelessly copy over some of my lines from [this](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/11564) discussion where the same point was raised:
> It never gave me trouble that multiple element types can emit change events; if for example I should need to listen at the document root and handle all change events from `<input>` elements only, I can differentiate simply by `e.target.tagName === 'INPUT'` and rely on this check being sufficient.
> But this isn't the case with the `toggle` events. [...]
> Currently the differentiation is difficult. Because popover is an attribute, a `<dialog>` or `<details>` can have the attribute. A `toggle` event originating in such an element is ambiguous.

> 3. Why is `event.cause == 'popover'` significantly better than `event.target.matches(':popover-open')` or `event.cause == 'dialog.modal'` better than `event.target.matches(':modal')`?

Having the proposed `event.cause` property would improve **all** cases to some extend; some cases only slightly, but other cases significantly.
Imagine you keep count on how many modal dialogs are open; when you receive a `toggle` event you check `event.newState === 'open' && event.target.matches(':modal')` to increase the counter; and you check `event.newState === 'closed' && event.target.matches(':modal')` to decrease the counter? But wait! In the latter case the dialog already closed, so `event.target.matches(':modal')` never matches. You can't tell from the event args what happened. So `event.cause === 'dialog.modal'` would for the first time in web history enable such a check, that is currently not possible without keeping references to all open modal dialogs.
But it would even improve the former check: because `event.newState === 'open' && event.target.matches(':modal')` is not strictly correct when we need to detect "opening modal dialogs". Any `<div>` that is turned to fullscreen would also match the `:modal` pseudo-class (although it would never trigger a toggle event and therefore never trigger our event listener).

The improvement is even more pronounced when you imagine you need to keep count on how many non-modal dialogs are open. How do we check for an "opening non-modal dialog"? We need to check like this:
`event.newState === 'open' && event.target.tagName === 'DIALOG' && !event.target.matches(':modal') && !event.target.matches(':popover-open')`
That is quite cumbersome. The new property would allow for a much shorter check: `event.newState === 'open'  && event.cause === 'dialog.default'`
(And the same impossibility to currently detect the closing case applies here also.)

> 4. Presumably even with `event.cause`, you're still going to maintain whatever branching model you have? Do you have a complete code example so we can see how it might look before and after? Does it save much time or effort or reduce complexity or potential bugs?

The proposed property would reduce complexity and potential bugs.

I haven't pushed my project to the github repo yet; usually I push a project only when it has reached the level of _first usable version_; I just started implementing when I fell down this rabbit hole of `toggle` event ambiguity.

Will try to push it soon and provide a link here.

**Update 2026-06-06**

The [repo](https://github.com/teetotum/top-layer-observer) of my top-layer-observer is now online.
The file that would change when `event.cause` would exist is the [core functions implementation file](https://github.com/teetotum/top-layer-observer/blob/master/src/core.js).

Relevant excerpts [Lines 90-111](https://github.com/teetotum/top-layer-observer/blob/master/src/core.js#L90-L111) and [Lines 167-187](https://github.com/teetotum/top-layer-observer/blob/master/src/core.js#L167-L187) (slightly simplified)

```js
    const causedByPopoverOpened = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'open' && event.target.matches(':popover-open');
    const causedByPopoverClosed = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'closed' && popovers.has(event.target);

    const causedByModalDialogOpened = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'open' && event.target.tagName === 'DIALOG' && event.target.matches(':modal');
    const causedByModalDialogClosed = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'closed' && event.target.tagName === 'DIALOG' && modalDialogs.has(event.target);

    const causedByNonModalDialogOpened = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'open' && event.target.tagName === 'DIALOG' && !event.target.matches(':modal')
        && !event.target.matches(':popover-open');
    const causedByNonModalDialogClosed = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'closed' && event.target.tagName === 'DIALOG' && nonModalDialogs.has(event.target);

    const causedByDetailsOpened = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'open' && event.target.tagName === 'DETAILS' && !event.target.matches(':popover-open');
    const causedByDetailsClosed = (event) =>
        event.newState === 'closed' && event.target.tagName === 'DETAILS' && details.has(event.target);

    const toggleEventHandler = (event) => {
        if (causedByPopoverOpened(event)) {
            handleEnterTopLayer(event.target, 'popover');
        } else if (causedByPopoverClosed(event)) {
            handleExitTopLayer(event.target, 'popover');
        } else if (causedByModalDialogOpened(event)) {
            handleEnterTopLayer(event.target, 'dialog.modal');
        } else if (causedByModalDialogClosed(event)) {
            handleExitTopLayer(event.target, 'dialog.modal');
        } else if (causedByNonModalDialogOpened(event)) {
            bookIn(event.target, 'dialog.default');
        } else if (causedByNonModalDialogClosed(event)) {
            bookOut(event.target, 'dialog.default');
        } else if (causedByDetailsOpened(event)) {
            bookIn(event.target, 'details.default');
        } else if (causedByDetailsClosed(event)) {
            bookOut(event.target, 'details.default');
        } else {
            console.warn(`Unhandled Case :: toggle event originated in ${event.target.tagName} from unknown cause.`)
        };
    };

    document.addEventListener('toggle', toggleEventHandler, { capture: true, signal: abortController.signal });
```

The excerpt above shows the code that currently does the case distinction for `toggle` events. It tries to cover all edge cases (probably still misses one or two?) and has grown quite complex.

With `event.cause` the code could be simplified into:

```js
    const toggleEventHandler = (event) => {
        switch (event.cause) {
            case 'popover': event.newState === 'open'
                ? handleEnterTopLayer(event.target, 'popover')
                : handleExitTopLayer(event.target, 'popover')
                break;
            case 'dialog.modal': event.newState === 'open'
                ? handleEnterTopLayer(event.target, 'dialog.modal')
                : handleExitTopLayer(event.target, 'dialog.modal')
                break;
        }
    };

    document.addEventListener('toggle', toggleEventHandler, { capture: true, signal: abortController.signal });
```
Sorry for the lengthy post. I did not intend to post a wall of text, just to plead my case as clear and diligent as I can manage.
